      "writtenOn": "2022-11-29T02:30:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Rust newbie\u0027s question back: ? seems to exit the current function (i.e. `run_vm`) immediately and propagate the error outside of the code block. Would you prefer to do so for invalid pvmfw path string? If so, then I\u0027d like to add logging.",
      "parentUuid": "de916f71_8483448f",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 608,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 608,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "eddc04649917824b0549df088ca7e536b3ae5937",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5025e3d5_6721f1bf",
        "filename": "virtualizationservice/src/crosvm.rs",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 608,
      "author": {
        "id": 1132673
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-11-29T04:33:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "failing to read the sysprop is a good reason to stop and exit. \n\nNot sure you want to add a log here because the error will anyway be propagated back to the caller and printed there. So, it is a good idea to add some context to the error message by using the .context() method:\n\n```\nif let Some(pvmfw_path) \u003d system_properties::read(\"...\")\n    .context(\"Failed to read xxxx\")? {\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nIf you want to do the logging at here, you could do as follows:\n\n```\nif let Some(pvmfw_path) \u003d system_properties::read(\"...\").map_err(|e| {\n   warning!(\"Failed to read xxxx: {:?}\", e);\n   e\n}\n```",
      "parentUuid": "9abb68d3_0786106d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 608,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 608,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "eddc04649917824b0549df088ca7e536b3ae5937",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8b02b616_f768d596",
        "filename": "virtualizationservice/src/crosvm.rs",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 608,
      "author": {
        "id": 1019013
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-11-29T11:42:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I meant to log invalid `pvmfw_path` and fallback to the `--protected-vm` as I did similar already. What do you think? I\u0027m asking this because both Jiyong and Pierre\u0027s suggestion seem to return immediately if `system_properties::read` returns Err(), while I think that continuing to launch VM seems acceptable. Correct me if I\u0027m wrong.",
      "parentUuid": "5025e3d5_6721f1bf",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 608,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 608,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "eddc04649917824b0549df088ca7e536b3ae5937",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "275ce89d_e11967bc",
        "filename": "virtualizationservice/src/crosvm.rs",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 608,
      "author": {
        "id": 1132673
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-11-29T13:13:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "There\u0027s no absolute answer here I believe. Both can be right. However, bailing on error is a common pattern found in AVF. We in general don\u0027t try to recover from an error, but to fail early and explicitly. This is helpful to find our potential issues while in development, and to prevent attackers from abusing the recovery behavior.",
      "parentUuid": "8b02b616_f768d596",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 608,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 608,
        "endChar": 13
      },
